/**
\page guidelines Programming Guidelines

As with all projects, the Yoyo project follows some programming guidelines.

\section guidelines_architecture Architectural Guidelines

At Yoyo, we follow the basic principles of object oriented analysis and object oriented design. We
aim for high cohesion and low coupling. This is best achieved by defining extremely narrow
interfaces not only to the user (SDK), but also within Yoyo itself.

For designing the classes and the interfaces, the SOLID principles were applied.
Whereever possible aggregation was used instead of inheritance (in particular to avoid diamond
inheritance graphs).

Yoyo is organized into modules, each
\section guidelines_coverage Test Driven Development

Yoyo strives for Test-Driven-Development. This means, that tests are written before the production
code is written. These tests fail, obviously. Then the minimum amount of code is written to make
these tests pass.

This helps keeping the code small (less lines of code means less code to maintain) and the tests can
act as a documentation for the intended behavior of the object under test.

When a bug occurrs, it is first reproduced with a test (preferably unit-test). This ensures, that
the bug has been understood and it verifies, that it had been resolved, when the test finally
passes.

When it comes to coverage, 85% line-coverage are aimed for.

\section guidelines_cpp Cpp Core Guidelines

Yoyo development tries to follow the Cpp Core Guidelines
(https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines). Some of the guidelines cannot be
applied though, being meant for the newest C++ version, while Yoyo is still C++-17.

\subsection guidelines_cpp_other General

- Use std::for_each preferably
- When in doubt choose stl types over Qt types (the API is much more stable)
- For Qt macros use the capitalized version: Q_SLOTS, Q_SIGNALS, Q_PROPERTY, Q_EMIT instead of
    slots, signals or emit.
- Own macros are allowed in tests and the test framework, nowhere else

Whenever you can have the compiler check your code instead of doing a runtime check, choose the
compiler to verify your code (strong typing). Example: Use std::chrono over int.

\section guidelines_style Coding Style

Yoyo uses clang-format to apply coding style. For the definition, see file ".clang-format" in the
top-level directory of the repository.

Clang-format is a state of the art tool for formatting code. It is provided as a by-product by LLVM,
the developers of the clang compiler (so it doesn't have issues processing "newer" C++ features,
since the compiler has to support them anyway).

\section guidelines_documentation Documenting the Code

The biggest rationale is: Don't document the obvious! But anything that isn't.

\subsection guidelines_documentation_interfaces Interfaces

Since the user of the code does not necessarily have access to the source code of the interface (in
particular true for the SDK and plugin developers), these headers must be documented with extreme
care, providing the user with all necessary information to use the classes and their methods
appropriately.

This includes in particular: input, output and side effects (like signal emission or throwing
exceptions).

\subsection guidelines_documentation_headers Package Private Headers

Headers of package private entities do not need to be documented as thoroughly as the interface
headers. At some points, the documentation may have already been applied to the base class.

Choose speaking names for the classes and functions you define. Further documentation is only
necessary, if there are non-obvious design decisions or similar.

Since the source code of the package private objects is next to the header files, anyone having
access to the header can easily look up the implementation for further information.

\subsection guidelines_documentation_sources Source Code

When documenting source code, document why implementation decisions have been made. Do not document
what is already written in code. The code may change, but rather often the comment describing, what
is done, doesn't, which leads to inconsistencies between code and comment.

Use Doxygen markers to make thoughts available in this documentation.
*/
